    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-ayodhya-kanda-0125",
      "sequence": 502,
      "meaning": "Rāma prostrated before the sage, and the excellent brahmin gave his blessing. His eyes were cooled by Rāma's beauty; honoring him, he brought the visitors into the hermitage. The great sage had received guests dearer than life and summoned sweet roots and fruit. Sītā, Sumitrā's son, and Rāma ate, and then the sage assigned them pleasant lodging in the hermitage. Vālmīki felt immense joy as his eyes gazed upon the embodiment of auspiciousness. Raghunātha joined his lotus hands and spoke words delightful to the ear: lord of sages, you see the three times; the universe lies in your hand like a jujube fruit. Saying this, the lord related the whole story of how the queen had sent him to the forest—his father's word, his mother's good, his brother Bharata, and the king's state. His sight of the sage, Rāma said, was all the blessed effect of his own merit.",
